8
00:00:32,652 --> 00:00:34,155
Rym: And we picked that.

9
00:00:34,195 --> 00:00:41,590
Rym: the reason we can afford to ski at Vail is mostly because I booked this place, like, seven months before we go.

10
00:00:42,210 --> 00:00:47,750
Rym: So it comes down to it's a lot cheaper, but we don't know what the weather is going to be like.

11
00:00:47,830 --> 00:00:51,770
Rym: I'm just kind of banking on late January, early February being a good time.

12
00:00:52,491 --> 00:00:59,730
Rym: Last year, we ended up being at Vail during one of the best weeks of powder snow they have ever had in recent history.

13
00:01:00,312 --> 00:01:05,590
Rym: And this year, we were there during what might have been the worst possible week.

14
00:01:05,852 --> 00:01:07,650
Rym: Because the previous week, there was tons of new snow.

15
00:01:08,152 --> 00:01:12,050
Rym: And there was a giant blizzard that dumped, like, eight inches of snow the day we left.

16
00:01:12,450 --> 00:01:17,370
Rym: And while we were there, it was 50 degrees in the fucking mountains of Colorado the whole week.

17
00:01:19,011 --> 00:01:31,570
Rym: But, you know, despite the conditions being bad by, like, fancy, rich person, West Coast skier sensibilities, in terms of being someone from the East Coast, I have now determined that it's not a joke.

18
00:01:31,810 --> 00:01:32,712
Rym: In fact, it's true.

19
00:01:33,675 --> 00:01:40,110
Rym: The worst day at Vail is better than the best day at any East Coast mountain that I have ever skied.

20
00:01:40,390 --> 00:01:41,618
Rym: That was perfectly fine.

21
00:01:42,061 --> 00:01:43,329
Rym: And the mountain was empty.

22
00:01:43,470 --> 00:01:44,533
Scott: And much higher up, of course.

23
00:01:44,593 --> 00:01:50,110
Scott: If there was a mountain over here that was that tall, then, you know, it just isn't.

24
00:01:50,370 --> 00:01:51,457
Scott: It's not that tall over here.

25
00:01:51,497 --> 00:01:53,609
Scott: The Appalachians are way old and got worn out.

26
00:01:54,172 --> 00:01:56,090
Scott: Like the Rockies, which are much younger and taller.

27
00:01:56,150 --> 00:01:58,638
Rym: Yeah, these punk kid mountains with their rocks everywhere.

28
00:01:58,678 --> 00:02:02,410
Rym: The Appalachians are just like grass-covered, rolling nonsense because they're so ancient.

29
00:02:04,710 --> 00:02:11,730
Rym: But there's always, you know, every year when we do these trips, two years in a row now, everyone on the trip is like, "Oh, you got to pace yourself.

30
00:02:12,630 --> 00:02:13,799
Rym: You're skiing too much.

31
00:02:13,820 --> 00:02:14,807
Rym: You got to tire yourself

32
00:02:14,968 --> 00:02:15,049
Rym: out.".

33
00:02:15,773 --> 00:02:19,330
Rym: And for two years in a row, every day I ski more and I feel better.

34
00:02:19,751 --> 00:02:21,455
Rym: My power is unlimited.

35
00:02:21,535 --> 00:02:24,983
Rym: And I skied, over the course of four days, 146 miles.

36
00:02:29,730 --> 00:02:30,515
Rym: It's a double down.

37
00:02:30,535 --> 00:02:32,206
Scott: You're not sideways or up skiing.

38
00:02:32,226 --> 00:02:32,910
Scott: You're down skiing.

39
00:02:32,990 --> 00:02:34,930
Rym: There's some sideways, like taking the catwalks around.

40
00:02:35,412 --> 00:02:37,746
Scott: And the hardest work is being done by the lift machine.

41
00:02:37,786 --> 00:02:38,530
Scott: Let's pick it full.

42
00:02:40,451 --> 00:02:44,890
Scott: It's like, if you skied up that much, you would be the greatest athlete in the history of the universe.

43
00:02:45,776 --> 00:02:47,526
Rym: There are people who are like, "You got to earn your

44
00:02:47,546 --> 00:02:47,848
Rym: turns.".

45
00:02:48,110 --> 00:02:52,810
Rym: And because a lot of ski resorts, you can ski down for free if you get up without using the lift.

46
00:02:52,890 --> 00:02:58,527
Rym: And there are people who will spend like all day hiking up a mountain to ski down at once.

47
00:02:58,568 --> 00:02:59,290
Rym: And that was their whole day.

48
00:03:00,270 --> 00:03:06,210
Scott: But I mean, if you enjoy the up part, then you're really just getting, you know, it's like going to a park, basically.

49
00:03:06,510 --> 00:03:07,470
Rym: And I do enjoy the up part.

50
00:03:07,710 --> 00:03:10,085
Scott: But if you only enjoy going down, well...

51
00:03:11,071 --> 00:03:12,836
Rym: I do really enjoy going down.

52
00:03:13,217 --> 00:03:17,450
Rym: But it was some fantastic skiing, despite the bad conditions, per West Coast standards.

53
00:03:18,431 --> 00:03:19,874
Scott: I like going up more than down.

54
00:03:19,934 --> 00:03:27,170
Scott: I can't climb with my hands, but if it's just feet only, like just walk up or bike up.

55
00:03:27,550 --> 00:03:29,956
Scott: I'd like to bike up and then take the lift down.

56
00:03:30,057 --> 00:03:31,982
Scott: Take the gondola down.

57
00:03:35,590 --> 00:03:36,155
Scott: Be like, "Oh, good.

58
00:03:36,175 --> 00:03:37,205
Scott: I don't have to do the dangerous

59
00:03:37,245 --> 00:03:37,407
Scott: part.".

60
00:03:37,790 --> 00:03:38,412
Rym: See, I like both.

61
00:03:38,432 --> 00:03:41,222
Rym: You know, you've seen me when we biked together on a downhill.

62
00:03:41,483 --> 00:03:43,430
Rym: I just don't touch my brakes.

63
00:03:43,990 --> 00:03:45,690
Rym: The faster I can go, the better on a downhill.

64
00:03:46,430 --> 00:03:50,570
Scott: Well, if you bike down too steep a downhill, you might hit the brakes.

65
00:03:51,050 --> 00:03:51,170
Rym: Yeah.

66
00:03:51,251 --> 00:03:58,790
Rym: Well, mountain biking often hit the great brakes, but not so much because of speed, but because there's something dicey in front of me and I don't want to hit it and have a problem.

67
00:03:59,991 --> 00:04:07,930
Rym: But the fastest I got skiing this time was 44.7 miles per hour, which is not the fastest I've ever skied, but it's up there.

68
00:04:08,170 --> 00:04:09,510
Scott: The gravity's doing most of the work, though.

69
00:04:09,770 --> 00:04:11,134
Rym: Yeah, but you've got to stay in control.

70
00:04:12,298 --> 00:04:15,890
Rym: If you just try to go straight down, you'll hit a speed higher than that.

71
00:04:16,390 --> 00:04:19,297
Rym: You'll probably die because you're not actually carving or steering.

72
00:04:19,798 --> 00:04:20,519
Rym: There's even signs.

73
00:04:20,539 --> 00:04:25,150
Rym: It's against the rules to straight line on big mountains because you'll just die.

74
00:04:26,270 --> 00:04:28,469
Rym: Snowboarders try to do it all the time and they get kicked out for it.

75
00:04:30,033 --> 00:04:32,390
Scott: So you're not allowed to just ski in a straight line?

76
00:04:32,450 --> 00:04:33,470
Rym: No, not straight down the mountain.

77
00:04:35,013 --> 00:04:36,590
Scott: What if it's big and wide open?

78
00:04:37,731 --> 00:04:40,175
Rym: The way skiing works, it's not like a sled.

79
00:04:40,515 --> 00:04:42,038
Rym: You're actually always carving.

80
00:04:42,278 --> 00:04:49,490
Rym: You're always either doing a special kind of slide maneuver or the edges of your skis are sort of carving like a rail through the snow.

81
00:04:49,530 --> 00:04:56,970
Scott: Yeah, but you watch Olympic downhill skiing and it's like they turn only when there's the ropes or the flags to make them stay on course.

82
00:04:57,090 --> 00:05:04,430
Rym: Watch them in slow motion and what you'll see is they always have an edge engage and when they don't, it's only in the brief period when they're transitioning from one edge to another.

83
00:05:05,930 --> 00:05:12,750
Scott: Yeah, but it's like if you said downhill skiing race and there wasn't any flags or ropes, it was just open, they would just go in a straight line.

84
00:05:13,050 --> 00:05:19,542
Rym: Well, you can actually get faster by edging even if it's in a straight line and that's not considered a straight line.

85
00:05:19,562 --> 00:05:23,109
Rym: Straight lining is people who go straight down without an edge engage the whole time.

86
00:05:25,311 --> 00:05:26,954
Rym: It's hard to explain unless you're a skier.

87
00:05:27,015 --> 00:05:27,696
Rym: You've never skied.

88
00:05:27,736 --> 00:05:34,890
Rym: so I don't think you really could feel the concept of what an edge means in skiing or snowboarding and it doesn't really have an analogy.

89
00:05:35,450 --> 00:05:38,210
Scott: You put the edge of the ski into the snow versus just not?

90
00:05:38,510 --> 00:05:41,578
Rym: Well, but it digs in and that's sort of part of the mechanics of skiing.

91
00:05:41,598 --> 00:05:46,630
Rym: and when you're going straight down, what you're actually doing is really quickly alternating between your two edges.

92
00:05:47,612 --> 00:05:51,150
Rym: It's like making hundreds and hundreds of tiny turns while you're going down.

93
00:05:51,871 --> 00:05:56,270
Scott: So if you don't do that, if you just stand and gravity just pulls you down.

94
00:05:56,510 --> 00:06:02,338
Rym: Yep, you'll actually have more of the ski touching the snow and you'll hit a lower maximum speed.

95
00:06:02,599 --> 00:06:04,462
Rym: You'll also not have an edge.

96
00:06:04,502 --> 00:06:04,902
Rym: engage.

97
00:06:04,942 --> 00:06:10,270
Rym: so if you do need to suddenly stop or turn, you can't quickly and if you try to engage too quickly, you might just fall over and die.

98
00:06:12,733 --> 00:06:14,830
Scott: I mean when they do the ski jump, they're just going straight.

99
00:06:15,051 --> 00:06:19,184
Rym: Well, the ski jump, they have one at Vail you can just do.

100
00:06:19,565 --> 00:06:20,367
Rym: Of course, I'm not crazy.

101
00:06:20,468 --> 00:06:21,110
Rym: I'm not doing that.

102
00:06:21,730 --> 00:06:22,347
Scott: You're not doing it?

103
00:06:22,571 --> 00:06:23,250
Scott: I'm not doing that.

104
00:06:23,450 --> 00:06:25,750
Scott: Do they have a baby's first ski jump?

105
00:06:26,691 --> 00:06:27,011
Rym: They do.

106
00:06:27,332 --> 00:06:31,583
