176
00:11:51,890 --> 00:11:55,916
Rym: So, there is some news, and it's one non-gaming news, but it's actually kind of important.

177
00:11:56,277 --> 00:12:02,746
Rym: We're not gonna dwell on it, but you don't know, the former president of the United States tried to stage a coup and steal the election.

178
00:12:03,167 --> 00:12:04,429
Rym: There's a whole thing on January 6th.

179
00:12:05,510 --> 00:12:21,870
Rym: And there was a lawsuit in the United States that went up all the way up to the federal courts and then was appealed, so this was an appeal in the US federal court, one or two, depending on how things proceed, levels below our Supreme Court.

180
00:12:22,550 --> 00:12:34,029
Rym: And the question at issue is basically, "Is a president of the United States permanently immune to all prosecution for all crimes under all circumstances or

181
00:12:34,110 --> 00:12:34,310
Rym: not?".

182
00:12:34,671 --> 00:12:40,170
Rym: And if that sounds ridiculous, well, welcome to America and our ridiculous Constitution.

183
00:12:41,490 --> 00:12:42,873
Rym: So, that's what's at stake.

184
00:12:42,993 --> 00:12:50,248
Rym: Literally, the question is, "Is a president of the United States permanently immune to all prosecution for all crimes forever, no matter

185
00:12:50,348 --> 00:12:50,508
Rym: what?".

186
00:12:51,513 --> 00:12:52,649
Scott: That they did while they were president.

187
00:12:54,031 --> 00:12:54,973
Scott: We're not counting crime.

188
00:12:55,093 --> 00:13:01,710
Scott: Obviously, no one's disputing if you did a crime when you weren't president, even if you became president or previously were president.

189
00:13:01,910 --> 00:13:05,430
Scott: If Obama right now shot someone, he would be arrested, right?

190
00:13:06,351 --> 00:13:10,205
Scott: The question is, "What if Obama shot someone immediately after being

191
00:13:10,245 --> 00:13:10,848
Scott: inaugurated?"?

192
00:13:10,888 --> 00:13:11,490
Scott: That's the question.

193
00:13:11,610 --> 00:13:19,230
Rym: Well, there is another case that effectively is a crime that was committed after the presidency, where there's a similar debate, but it's lower down in the courts right now.

194
00:13:19,671 --> 00:13:21,617
Rym: There's so many, no one can keep track.

195
00:13:21,677 --> 00:13:26,110
Rym: There's like 96 different charges across America just on this one guy.

196
00:13:26,310 --> 00:13:34,550
Rym: But anyway, the federal court has in fact ruled that presidents are not immune to criminal prostitution.

197
00:13:34,790 --> 00:13:35,076
Scott: Oh, damn.

198
00:13:35,096 --> 00:13:35,850
Scott: That's disappointing.

199
00:13:36,530 --> 00:13:46,768
Rym: Yeah, considering that had they ruled that presidents are immune, then the current president could legally steal the election, or legally punch the former president in the nuts.

200
00:13:47,409 --> 00:13:48,190
Rym: Just fully legally.

201
00:13:49,012 --> 00:13:58,270
Scott: I mean, effectively, if you say that the president is immune from being prosecuted for any crimes, you're basically saying it's not a democracy, right?

202
00:13:58,470 --> 00:14:06,269
Scott: Because you're effectively an autocrat because you can just, right, it's like if people won't do what you say, well you can just shoot them.

203
00:14:07,532 --> 00:14:11,910
Scott: But if they shoot you, that's the highest possible crime there is, so they wouldn't do that.

204
00:14:12,190 --> 00:14:26,390
Rym: So this decision wasn't just like the obvious decision, but this appeals court in the United States, they wrote the most watertight, intense ruling that I've ever read cover to cover.

205
00:14:26,610 --> 00:14:30,496
Rym: It basically is like, there is absolutely no way to overturn this.

206
00:14:30,836 --> 00:14:39,890
Rym: Here is the ridiculous list of insane consequences if we ruled the other way, and here is mountains of evidence as to why this is an absolutely stupid claim.

207
00:14:40,770 --> 00:14:41,188
Scott: That's right.

208
00:14:43,234 --> 00:14:45,069
Scott: Stupid common sense, taking away all our fun.

209
00:14:45,470 --> 00:14:45,610
Scott: Yep.

210
00:14:46,691 --> 00:15:02,990
Rym: So now what's going to be really interesting is either the former president of the United States who tried to stage a coup can appeal this to a larger federal court, which will almost definitely rule the exact same way, or he can appeal it to the Supreme Court.

211
00:15:04,550 --> 00:15:16,370
Rym: And it is hard, considering the way this decision was written, to imagine any universe where even the most corrupt Supreme Court would take this case up and overturn it.

212
00:15:16,490 --> 00:15:32,090
Rym: Because if they did that, it would require them to basically say, we don't give a shit, and we're just going to arbitrarily rule and ignore everything that the rest of the court system said, which would probably lead to one of the first crises of the courts that the United States has ever had.

213
00:15:32,891 --> 00:15:49,610
Scott: Well, it would also, like we said, mean that Joe Biden is a dictator, and can just, can have those, they'd basically be saying, yes, if Joe Biden sends assassins to kill us and burn our houses down and et cetera, et cetera, no one could do anything about it.

214
00:15:49,650 --> 00:15:55,090
Scott: And I don't think anyone would say that if they weren't his friend, and some of them are not.

215
00:15:55,290 --> 00:15:55,992
Rym: So, yep.

216
00:15:56,092 --> 00:16:02,730
Rym: Now, I think there are three of them who would say that, but they qualify that only if it's a Republican president.

217
00:16:04,772 --> 00:16:10,250
Scott: But he's not a Republican president right now, and they have to decide before it has a chance to be.

218
00:16:11,251 --> 00:16:12,354
Rym: So I'm going to make a prediction.

219
00:16:12,374 --> 00:16:18,070
Rym: I think our cowardly Supreme Court is just going to refuse to take this case up.

220
00:16:18,574 --> 00:16:19,709
Scott: Yeah, that's what they do most of the time.

221
00:16:19,770 --> 00:16:28,450
Rym: Yep, because then in American law, the lower court's ruling stands, but it does not set a precedent except in the jurisdiction of that appeals court.

222
00:16:28,910 --> 00:16:43,070
Rym: So it lets the Supreme Court, which is super corrupt and has basically zero faith in America right now, dodge this and try to not get involved, even though it's kind of a moment where you really need the highest court in the land to also say the common sense thing.

223
00:16:43,550 --> 00:16:47,470
Scott: That's fine, because it means he's not immune and he'll get in trouble for all his crimes.

224
00:16:47,610 --> 00:16:48,272
Rym: Exactly.

225
00:16:48,813 --> 00:16:50,738
Rym: So this is good news.

226
00:16:50,918 --> 00:16:55,570
Rym: The process somehow is slowly, possibly working.

227
00:16:57,785 --> 00:16:57,930
Scott: Anyway.

228
00:16:58,230 --> 00:17:01,790
Rym: Yeah, anyway, in some other news, you got some news?

229
00:17:03,132 --> 00:17:04,002
Scott: I did have a news.

230
00:17:04,123 --> 00:17:04,690
Scott: What was it?

231
00:17:04,869 --> 00:17:05,869
Rym: The translation model?

232
00:17:06,731 --> 00:17:07,131
Scott: Oh, yeah.

233
00:17:07,151 --> 00:17:16,310
Scott: So Roblox, which is a child labor machine that you should not get involved with, has been the first to do a thing that's sort of obvious.

234
00:17:16,892 --> 00:17:17,132
Scott: Right.

235
00:17:17,473 --> 00:17:18,135
Scott: But you know what?

236
00:17:18,676 --> 00:17:22,990
Scott: Doing an obvious thing first is still pretty meaningful.

237
00:17:23,571 --> 00:17:28,109
Scott: And what they've done is they've taken a machine learning translator model.

238
00:17:28,451 --> 00:17:28,733
Scott: Right.

239
00:17:29,295 --> 00:17:32,530
Scott: Which there are many of them, you know, Duolingo's got one.

240
00:17:33,272 --> 00:17:41,350
Scott: Plenty of people got one and they've gotten the performance of it and the accuracy of it high enough to where they find it acceptable.

241
00:17:42,212 --> 00:17:47,430
Scott: And they're allowing you to apply it to just live the live chat in Roblox.

242
00:17:48,090 --> 00:18:04,570
Scott: So if you're on a Roblox game server, whatever, and you're in there with some Russians and some Chinese people and some Koreans and some Americans and some French people and some Guyanese people and some Argentinian people, you can all chat.

243
00:18:05,190 --> 00:18:19,710
Scott: And in the text box, all the chat, 100 percent of it will be in your native language of choice, even though all the participants in the chat are from around the world in near-ish real chat time, at least.

244
00:18:20,030 --> 00:18:23,010
Scott: You know, not that IRC chat is real time, but fast enough.

245
00:18:24,411 --> 00:18:29,990
Scott: And thus provide, you know, the connection across the world we've all been seeking.

246
00:18:31,932 --> 00:18:35,590
Scott: And it's like, hey, good job doing that first.

247
00:18:35,790 --> 00:18:37,410
Scott: Everyone else is going to do it eventually.

248
00:18:37,830 --> 00:18:41,129
Rym: I think it's nothing but good news, because even if you wanted it.

249
00:18:41,350 --> 00:18:46,710
Rym: So if you want to make an argument that AI will take away jobs, well, human translators could not do this.

250
00:18:47,252 --> 00:18:49,368
Rym: No number of human translators could do this.

251
00:18:50,172 --> 00:18:51,176
Scott: No.

252
00:18:51,317 --> 00:18:54,810
Scott: And it's, you know, it's not going to be as good, clearly, as human translators.

253
00:18:55,071 --> 00:18:57,890
Scott: And when you need good, you're going to still get a human translator.

254
00:18:58,270 --> 00:19:01,970
Rym: The United Nations is going to use human translators for the foreseeable future.

255
00:19:03,850 --> 00:19:04,011
Scott: Right.

256
00:19:04,432 --> 00:19:08,890
Scott: But, you know, hey, this, you know, hopefully other apps apply it.

257
00:19:09,112 --> 00:19:10,469
Scott: But if they don't, you know what?

258
00:19:10,831 --> 00:19:18,010
Scott: It could be worth it to install Roblox, not to do any Roblox or to engage in child labor or to buy any skins or get Robux.

259
00:19:18,050 --> 00:19:20,430
Rym: Just to use the Star Trek style universal translator.

260
00:19:20,951 --> 00:19:23,810
Scott: Just to go in the chat room and talk to people.

261
00:19:24,010 --> 00:19:35,970
Scott: It's like, you know, wait, what if you got to, you know, you're working on some open source project and someone else is, you know, tries to get in your GitHub issues and they're talking to you.

262
00:19:36,430 --> 00:19:37,112
Scott: But they're tight.

263
00:19:37,133 --> 00:19:41,950
Scott: You know, they clearly English is not their first language or your native language is not their first language.

264
00:19:41,990 --> 00:19:44,422
Scott: Be like, hey, meet me and meet me in Roblox.

265
00:19:45,126 --> 00:19:45,870
Scott: Let's chat there.

266
00:19:46,351 --> 00:19:52,990
Scott: And, you know, it'll be a lot just the fact that you could just copy paste to Google Translate or use all kinds of other mechanisms.

267
00:19:53,210 --> 00:19:57,110
Rym: I've done that before, but it's tedious and slow and it really messes up.

268
00:19:57,290 --> 00:20:03,469
Scott: But just having the chat room automatically do it just, you know, makes the UX of it.

269
00:20:04,452 --> 00:20:05,630
Scott: It's a dramatic difference.

270
00:20:06,115 --> 00:20:07,149
Scott: It's an obvious idea.

271
00:20:08,672 --> 00:20:10,790
Scott: Everyone knew this was coming from somebody.

272
00:20:11,310 --> 00:20:14,409
Scott: But Roblox can say they were the first big one to do it, at least.

273
00:20:15,331 --> 00:20:16,687
Scott: And I hope Discord does it.

274
00:20:17,550 --> 00:20:21,369
Scott: And Slack and an IRC plug in.

275
00:20:22,631 --> 00:20:29,510
Rym: And yeah, because even if it's clearly not perfect and there's, we'll need more time for this kind of machine.

276
00:20:29,990 --> 00:20:30,910
Scott: It doesn't have to be perfect.

277
00:20:30,991 --> 00:20:32,190
Scott: It has to be good enough, right?

278
00:20:33,673 --> 00:20:38,382
Scott: A lot of people out there keep, you know, thinking about like, you know, the old U.M.

279
00:20:38,402 --> 00:20:41,968
Scott: play God sci fi stories of like, you know, the robot.

280
00:20:43,978 --> 00:20:44,669
Scott: It did it wrong.

281
00:20:45,290 --> 00:20:46,510
Scott: That's why we can't trust robot.

282
00:20:47,332 --> 00:20:52,370
Scott: But it's like, you know, I say the same thing about cars that drive on their own, but no human drivers.

283
00:20:52,650 --> 00:20:54,870
Scott: It's like, look, the standard isn't perfect.

284
00:20:55,050 --> 00:20:59,889
Scott: We don't need a car that drives itself that never hits anyone as nice as that would be.

285
00:21:00,351 --> 00:21:04,590
Scott: It just has to hit as many or fewer people than humans do.

286
00:21:04,750 --> 00:21:06,070
Scott: And then it will save lives.

287
00:21:06,654 --> 00:21:08,490
Scott: And you should do it even if it's not perfect.

288
00:21:08,991 --> 00:21:22,030
Scott: If the translating chat room is good enough to where people who do not share a common spoken language can communicate with each other meaningfully and bring together humanity in some small way.

289
00:21:22,413 --> 00:21:23,789
Scott: It's good enough to start using it.

290
00:21:23,890 --> 00:21:24,231
Scott: Yeah.

291
00:21:24,331 --> 00:21:32,430
Rym: Think about the cultural exchange that's about to happen among all the kids in Roblox around the world over the next year like that.

292
00:21:32,710 --> 00:21:40,070
Scott: I just don't know how popular the Roblox is in the other parts of the world, but it must be popular at least more than one part of the world if they're bothering to put this in.

293
00:21:40,350 --> 00:21:46,390
Rym: Based on my limited looking into Roblox, it seems like it is popular in many, many countries.

294
00:21:47,110 --> 00:21:47,372
Scott: I'm sure.

295
00:21:47,412 --> 00:21:50,470
Scott: I just wonder, you know, there's probably some countries where it's not so popular.

296
00:21:52,796 --> 00:21:53,849
Scott: It's not every country.

297
00:21:56,591 --> 00:21:59,430
Scott: A lot of countries I imagine don't allow child labor.

298
00:21:59,610 --> 00:22:04,770
Rym: Before long, we're going to have this in near real time for spoken word where you don't even hear the other person's voice.

299
00:22:05,450 --> 00:22:15,430
Scott: The only question is, right, is that these sorts of, you know, the current paradigm of machine learning from a computing perspective is very, you know, resource intensive.

300
00:22:16,032 --> 00:22:17,958
Scott: And so who is paying for this?

301
00:22:18,219 --> 00:22:19,563
Scott: and, you know, the energy cost?

302
00:22:19,623 --> 00:22:21,610
Scott: It's not near the energy cost of coins.

303
00:22:23,191 --> 00:22:31,505
Scott: But it's like, is Roblox going to cost the money to run this thing and will they make that money back or will they say, "Ah, this is too expensive.

304
00:22:31,545 --> 00:22:34,129
Scott: We can't, you know, be paying for

305
00:22:34,169 --> 00:22:34,310
Scott: this.".

306
00:22:34,470 --> 00:22:34,731
Rym: Yeah.

307
00:22:35,032 --> 00:22:39,290
Rym: Of course, this kind of model is the kind of model that could become very efficient over time.

308
00:22:40,812 --> 00:22:43,570
Rym: But I guess I think that's the conversation for another day, but this is big news.

309
00:22:43,610 --> 00:22:47,044
Scott: I think it could work well as if it's like a paid plugin, right?

310
00:22:47,064 --> 00:22:47,747
Scott: Like, you know.

311
00:22:49,530 --> 00:22:55,167
Scott: Like, yeah, Discord, you can use it if your server is all boosted and full of nitros and boosts and whatnot, right?

312
00:22:55,508 --> 00:22:56,049
Scott: But otherwise, no.

313
00:23:02,470 --> 00:23:05,634
Rym: But anyway, things of the day.

314
00:23:05,735 --> 00:23:17,730
Rym: So I haven't linked to SciShow in a long time and I'm linking to it now only because there are two kinds of arguments around keyboards that come up kind of often in my life across many social circles.

315
00:23:18,230 --> 00:23:21,504
Scott: That's just because you argue with people when they talk about keyboards.

316
00:23:22,047 --> 00:23:22,770
Scott: Stop doing that.

317
00:23:23,190 --> 00:23:24,714
Rym: I like arguing with people about keyboards.

318
00:23:25,476 --> 00:23:31,010
Rym: But there's the argument around, are there keyboard layouts that are better than other keyboard layouts?

319
00:23:31,230 --> 00:23:36,850
Rym: Which kind of stupid argument, partly because we do have a lot of data on it and QWERTY is not the best.

320
00:23:37,371 --> 00:23:43,710
Rym: But of the ones that are commonly used for people who type in English, it's the fastest and it's kind of naturally good.

321
00:23:43,830 --> 00:23:44,975
Scott: Don't care if it's the best.

322
00:23:45,096 --> 00:23:46,763
Scott: That's what you're going to encounter.

323
00:23:46,824 --> 00:23:47,748
Scott: So that's how you should learn.

324
00:23:47,788 --> 00:23:48,230
Scott: Deal with it.

325
00:23:48,270 --> 00:23:52,510
Rym: Like learning Dvorak is literally just a waste of your time and it's just going to fuck up your life.

326
00:23:53,450 --> 00:23:59,590
Rym: But the other one is people who love mechanical keyboards versus people who weirdly hate mechanical keyboards.

327
00:24:00,030 --> 00:24:02,374
Scott: I haven't met too many people who hate the mechanical keyboards.

328
00:24:02,435 --> 00:24:10,570
Scott: It's really people who care about keyboards and thus get crazy with the mechanical keyboards and then people who just really don't give a shit or don't even type.

329
00:24:10,811 --> 00:24:12,014
Rym: So that was what was fascinating.

330
00:24:12,034 --> 00:24:17,770
Scott: I haven't seen many people who type, care about typing and hate mechanical keyboards and really like membranes.

331
00:24:18,430 --> 00:24:20,113
Rym: So that was what was fascinating.

332
00:24:20,414 --> 00:24:29,250
Rym: In this study, the one thing that came out that was not expected was that most people don't really have opinions on keyboards of any type.

333
00:24:29,370 --> 00:24:31,234
Scott: Yeah, because they're not thinking about it.

334
00:24:31,254 --> 00:24:31,314
Rym: Yep.

335
00:24:31,334 --> 00:24:38,130
Rym: But if you ask anyone about their opinion on mechanical keyboards, almost nobody asked has no opinion.

336
00:24:38,652 --> 00:24:44,330
Rym: People cluster into extreme like and extreme dislike and almost nothing in the middle, even regular basic people.

337
00:24:44,350 --> 00:24:46,676
Scott: Do the people who dislike even know what the hell you were talking about?

338
00:24:46,757 --> 00:24:51,530
Rym: They identify them as the loud keyboard that my annoying coworker uses.

339
00:24:51,910 --> 00:24:52,471
Scott: Yeah, but they're right.

340
00:24:52,511 --> 00:24:53,013
Scott: But they don't.

341
00:24:53,053 --> 00:24:53,233
Scott: Right.

342
00:24:53,273 --> 00:24:56,180
Scott: So obviously, I also hate the noise of a mechanical keyboard.

343
00:24:56,200 --> 00:24:56,401
Scott: Right.

344
00:24:56,441 --> 00:25:00,270
Scott: But it's like so that person is a person who heard their neighbor's annoying keyboard.

345
00:25:01,790 --> 00:25:10,510
Scott: Rightly was annoyed by it and thus never considered using one or being a person who uses one because they don't even think about why, you know, the feel of it or anything.

346
00:25:10,630 --> 00:25:12,896
Scott: They're just like, don't want to be that annoying, noisy guy.

347
00:25:13,237 --> 00:25:15,684
Rym: So there are three, I think, key takeaways from the study.

348
00:25:15,724 --> 00:25:17,750
Rym: You can watch the video for the rest, but it's pretty good.

349
00:25:18,231 --> 00:25:26,582
Rym: Takeaway number one is, yes, mechanical keyboards with the two stages of pressure just do make you type faster.

350
00:25:26,742 --> 00:25:31,669
Rym: There's strong evidence you will just type faster on a mechanical keyboard with fewer errors.

351
00:25:31,969 --> 00:25:32,470
Rym: Full stop.

352
00:25:32,931 --> 00:25:34,312
Rym: Like that is repeatable science.

353
00:25:34,853 --> 00:25:35,274
Rym: So sure.

354
00:25:35,594 --> 00:25:47,370
Rym: Two, membrane keyboards and tablets seem to be much more likely to cause carpal tunnel and other issues because they provide less travel.

355
00:25:47,550 --> 00:25:49,694
Rym: And that travel does appear to be necessary.

356
00:25:49,774 --> 00:25:51,878
Scott: Is this paid for by big keyboard switch?

357
00:25:52,119 --> 00:25:54,043
Scott: No, it's a pretty independent study.

358
00:25:54,524 --> 00:25:55,686
Rym: Gatorade ran this study.

359
00:25:58,311 --> 00:26:00,474
Rym: But the third takeaway was fascinating.

360
00:26:00,875 --> 00:26:09,930
Rym: The third takeaway was that the sound is in no way related to the typing accuracy and speed increases in mechanical keyboards.

361
00:26:10,290 --> 00:26:10,932
Scott: Of course not.

362
00:26:10,952 --> 00:26:13,157
Scott: It's just annoying to make annoying noises.

363
00:26:13,237 --> 00:26:15,663
Rym: I actually wish my mechanical keyboard was silent.

364
00:26:15,703 --> 00:26:16,245
Rym: It's just not an option.

365
00:26:16,265 --> 00:26:18,310
Scott: You could have gotten different keys that are silent.

366
00:26:18,370 --> 00:26:18,711
Rym: No, no.

367
00:26:18,951 --> 00:26:28,650
Rym: So the ones that are the style of the Cherry MX Blue, all the ones that have the two, like that particular power curve or a similar one, those are the fastest ones for typing.

368
00:26:29,670 --> 00:26:40,544
Rym: But if you make that same feeling with no sound somehow, which is very hard to do apparently, then you get like almost the same increase.

369
00:26:40,804 --> 00:26:49,175
Rym: But meanwhile, if you have a keyboard that has the same travel but doesn't have that pressure gulf, no matter what sound it makes, it doesn't make you faster.

370
00:26:50,617 --> 00:26:57,128
Scott: There are several keyboard switches you can get from like the Boba ones and the Kaai.

371
00:26:57,188 --> 00:26:58,150
Scott: I don't know how to pronounce it.

372
00:26:58,190 --> 00:27:02,362
Rym: Honestly, the only reason I have Cherry MX Blues is that I bought this keyboard so long ago.

373
00:27:02,402 --> 00:27:04,307
Rym: That was really the only one that had that feeling.

374
00:27:04,327 --> 00:27:05,150
Rym: There's a bunch more now.

375
00:27:05,932 --> 00:27:07,618
Scott: Box Mute Jade or something.

376
00:27:07,638 --> 00:27:10,830
Scott: There's all these other keyboard switch brands out there nowadays.

377
00:27:11,970 --> 00:27:19,210
Rym: If this keyboard ever dies, I am definitely going to try to find the best mechanical feel with the least possible sound.

378
00:27:19,750 --> 00:27:21,996
Scott: So the keyboard that I got, I upgraded my keyboard.

379
00:27:22,557 --> 00:27:26,267
Scott: I didn't want to because my other keyboard was working absolutely perfectly.

380
00:27:26,307 --> 00:27:27,210
Scott: There was nothing wrong with it.

381
00:27:27,530 --> 00:27:31,136
Scott: But I was solving two problems with this new keyboard.

382
00:27:32,818 --> 00:27:34,541
Scott: One, wireless, which is nice.

383
00:27:34,721 --> 00:27:40,390
Scott: And two, because it's wireless, I can press a key to switch between three different Bluetooth devices.

384
00:27:41,951 --> 00:27:43,174
Scott: It's sort of like a KVM.

385
00:27:43,855 --> 00:27:46,862
Scott: And I can switch between Mac and PC with one switch on the back.

386
00:27:47,424 --> 00:27:50,070
Scott: So that's why I got a new keyboard.

387
00:27:50,210 --> 00:27:53,977
Scott: But this new keyboard actually has hot sloppable switches.

388
00:27:54,398 --> 00:27:58,606
Scott: So I can go and buy a sack of switches and just pop them in and out.

389
00:27:58,667 --> 00:28:00,350
Scott: I can have different switches and different keys.

390
00:28:02,752 --> 00:28:08,447
Scott: So I'm considering actually doing that because even though I had, you know, my previous keyboard, I couldn't take the switches out.

391
00:28:08,488 --> 00:28:09,330
Scott: They were soldered in.

392
00:28:10,751 --> 00:28:19,327
Scott: And that one had Cherry MX Browns and this one has Keychron K Browns, which is supposed to be the same, but I don't think they're the same.

393
00:28:19,367 --> 00:28:19,988
Scott: They're kind of eh.

394
00:28:21,190 --> 00:28:24,870
Rym: Because I got the Browns at work and they feel like 90 percent as good as the Blues.

395
00:28:25,850 --> 00:28:26,572
Scott: Yeah, no.

396
00:28:26,632 --> 00:28:28,795
Scott: I've been all in on Brown for a while.

397
00:28:28,815 --> 00:28:30,819
Scott: It's the most popular one.

398
00:28:31,781 --> 00:28:34,586
Scott: But I might, you know, swap it.

399
00:28:36,709 --> 00:28:37,030
Scott: We'll see.

400
00:28:37,210 --> 00:28:37,431
Scott: Yeah.

401
00:28:37,451 --> 00:28:43,730
Rym: It's hard for me to justify because I don't have that switching use case and my DOS keyboard seems to be literally invulnerable.

402
00:28:44,830 --> 00:28:44,910
Scott: Yeah.

403
00:28:44,931 --> 00:28:48,649
Scott: I mean, if you don't know, the switching use case is really the thing, right?

404
00:28:48,910 --> 00:28:49,110
Rym: Yeah.

405
00:28:50,013 --> 00:28:56,930
Rym: If I had that capability, though, I could definitely see putting higher pressure on like enter and a couple other things.

406
00:28:58,294 --> 00:29:00,089
Rym: Though I do have blacks on the spacebar.

407
00:29:02,931 --> 00:29:08,370
Scott: Anyway, a lot of times keyboard people put different, you know, switches on the spacebar and such.

408
00:29:08,610 --> 00:29:08,691
Rym: Yeah.

409
00:29:08,711 --> 00:29:11,470
Rym: Well, because you're hitting the spacebar a lot and you're hitting it hard.

410
00:29:12,213 --> 00:29:12,354
Scott: Yeah.

411
00:29:12,374 --> 00:29:13,566
Scott: And it's different shape.

412
00:29:13,606 --> 00:29:13,950
Scott: It's weird.

413
00:29:15,030 --> 00:29:16,189
Rym: So anyway, you got a thing of the day?

414
00:29:17,091 --> 00:29:17,452
Scott: I do.

415
00:29:17,552 --> 00:29:19,897
Scott: So it is Black History Month.

416
00:29:19,937 --> 00:29:21,100
Scott: Well, it's the.

417
00:29:21,280 --> 00:29:25,410
Scott: every month is the Black History Month, but this is the very Black History Month February.

418
00:29:26,392 --> 00:29:28,510
Scott: It's like one of the only good things going on in February.

419
00:29:30,932 --> 00:29:34,810
Scott: And so someone posted a very interesting thing on Board Game Geek.

420
00:29:35,210 --> 00:29:36,839
Scott: It's not very it's not very big.

421
00:29:37,040 --> 00:29:38,809
Scott: It's just a little a little a little post.

422
00:29:40,311 --> 00:29:43,424
Scott: And I was like, oh, I wish I would have thought of that.

423
00:29:43,484 --> 00:29:44,930
Scott: Thanks for making me think of that.

424
00:29:45,050 --> 00:29:50,310
Scott: Which is, you know, in my life and my family, there were some card games that we played.

425
00:29:50,590 --> 00:29:53,070
Scott: Yeah, we played a Rummy, you know, five card draw.

426
00:29:53,512 --> 00:29:53,774
Rym: Right.

427
00:29:53,894 --> 00:29:54,739
Rym: We played mostly.

428
00:29:54,759 --> 00:29:55,584
Rym: you play hearts.

429
00:29:55,765 --> 00:29:56,509
Scott: You played a Euchre.

430
00:29:56,830 --> 00:29:57,111
Scott: Right.

431
00:29:57,673 --> 00:30:04,790
Scott: And it's like, yeah, of course, every different kind of person in every different kind of culture around the world has some kind of game.

432
00:30:04,910 --> 00:30:05,996
Scott: They're playing with their family.

433
00:30:06,036 --> 00:30:06,920
Scott: We know the Japanese.

434
00:30:06,960 --> 00:30:09,010
Scott: They're playing the Hanafuda because we watch summer wars.

435
00:30:09,431 --> 00:30:09,673
Scott: Right.

436
00:30:10,196 --> 00:30:11,242
Scott: And we know about Nintendo.

437
00:30:11,885 --> 00:30:12,469
Scott: But you know what?

438
00:30:13,591 --> 00:30:15,863
Scott: What were the black people playing in their families?

439
00:30:15,924 --> 00:30:17,110
Scott: What card games are they playing?

440
00:30:17,250 --> 00:30:17,774
Scott: And you know what?

441
00:30:18,277 --> 00:30:20,049
Scott: I'm sure all the black people all know.

442
00:30:20,632 --> 00:30:22,298
Scott: And us white people, we don't know.

443
00:30:22,559 --> 00:30:22,840
Scott: Right.

444
00:30:23,764 --> 00:30:25,350
Scott: We don't know what card games they were playing.

445
00:30:25,550 --> 00:30:28,117
Scott: And this person just posted a board game geek, Grayfax.

446
00:30:28,258 --> 00:30:32,430
Scott: And they're like, hey, these are the card games that our black family played.

447
00:30:32,590 --> 00:30:35,499
Scott: We played a game called Tonk, which is Rummy ish.

448
00:30:36,261 --> 00:30:37,164
Scott: They played with.

449
00:30:37,204 --> 00:30:39,110
Scott: they played bridge and spades, which we know.

450
00:30:39,792 --> 00:30:40,776
Scott: And they played bit.

451
00:30:40,836 --> 00:30:42,322
Scott: They played whist with bidding.

452
00:30:42,342 --> 00:30:43,185
Rym: Oh, yeah.

453
00:30:44,511 --> 00:30:50,125
Scott: So it was interesting to read about the games they played, especially this talk game.

454
00:30:50,145 --> 00:30:51,890
Scott: that was new to me.

455
00:30:52,191 --> 00:30:54,277
Scott: The other ones were most, you know, not so new to me.

456
00:30:54,357 --> 00:30:58,350
Scott: But I was like, oh, I guess that's the games that are popular with their family.

457
00:30:58,911 --> 00:31:01,400
Scott: And I think that gives you, you know, good insight.

458
00:31:01,420 --> 00:31:04,170
Scott: That's like that's like real insight into what someone's culture is.

459
00:31:04,250 --> 00:31:04,431
Scott: Right.

460
00:31:04,491 --> 00:31:08,730
Scott: Not the you know, people when they think about other cultures, it's like, what kind of food do they make?

461
00:31:08,870 --> 00:31:09,834
Scott: What's their fashion?

462
00:31:09,895 --> 00:31:10,618
Scott: What's their music?

463
00:31:10,678 --> 00:31:11,803
Scott: What's their dancing?

464
00:31:11,863 --> 00:31:12,044
Scott: Right.

465
00:31:12,084 --> 00:31:13,310
Scott: It's all these big things.

466
00:31:13,531 --> 00:31:14,072
Scott: You can see.

467
00:31:14,434 --> 00:31:18,206
Scott: But those are things that people are doing it like holidays and festivals.

468
00:31:18,427 --> 00:31:18,548
Scott: Right.

469
00:31:18,568 --> 00:31:19,250
Scott: And those are rare.

470
00:31:19,390 --> 00:31:22,021
Rym: What are what is the family doing together on a Tuesday?

471
00:31:22,703 --> 00:31:22,864
Scott: Right.

472
00:31:22,924 --> 00:31:24,330
Scott: It's like the everyday stuff.

473
00:31:24,410 --> 00:31:24,852
Scott: It's like that.

474
00:31:24,892 --> 00:31:30,550
Scott: I feel like that tells you more about what's, you know, someone's culture and you feel closer to them.

475
00:31:30,670 --> 00:31:36,790
Scott: So I just love to hear this, even though it was just a little tiny blog post on a board game geek forum.

476
00:31:38,351 --> 00:31:40,964
Rym: In the meta moment, the GeekNights book club book is.

477
00:31:41,286 --> 00:31:42,150
Rym: the night is short.

478
00:31:42,570 --> 00:31:43,594
Rym: Walk on, girl.

479
00:31:43,936 --> 00:31:45,764
Rym: The novel that inspired the movie.

480
00:31:45,804 --> 00:31:46,447
Rym: The night is short.

481
00:31:46,527 --> 00:31:47,150
Rym: Walk on, girl.

482
00:31:47,310 --> 00:31:48,554
Rym: I've read about a third of it.

483
00:31:49,297 --> 00:31:50,762
Rym: It moves pretty quick.

484
00:31:51,063 --> 00:31:52,949
Scott: I read until the first part.

485
00:31:53,350 --> 00:31:54,635
Scott: It's like, ah, end of part one.

486
00:31:54,656 --> 00:31:55,399
Scott: And I have to go back.

487
00:31:56,483 --> 00:31:57,307
Rym: I am enjoying it.

488
00:31:57,367 --> 00:31:58,010
Rym: It's a short read.

489
00:31:58,170 --> 00:31:59,055
Rym: I think it's worth reading.

490
00:31:59,155 --> 00:32:02,030
Rym: If any of you, for whatever reason, aren't reading it yet.

491
00:32:02,271 --> 00:32:05,790
Rym: Well, remember, we just did an episode on the murder about diaries.

492
00:32:06,171 --> 00:32:11,950
Rym: And those are some of the fastest reads you're going to find in like modern popular science fiction.

493
00:32:13,710 --> 00:32:20,930
Rym: Otherwise, we are going to be live at PAX East with a brand new panel, which we are still currently writing.

494
00:32:21,230 --> 00:32:22,775
Rym: And in fact, this episode is us.

495
00:32:22,815 --> 00:32:27,870
Rym: We're going to start to riff a little bit on some of the aspects that may be included in this panel.

496
00:32:28,231 --> 00:32:30,859
Rym: But every game is the same game.

497
00:32:31,119 --> 00:32:34,610
Rym: I'll post the schedule once they finalize it, but it'll be at PAX East.

498
00:32:35,350 --> 00:32:38,970
Rym: Otherwise, we're just going to be at PAX East for the whole time, hanging out, playing games.

499
00:32:39,090 --> 00:32:46,590
Rym: If you're there, there's a chance we might see you briefly as we pass in the wind in the probably extremely crowded tabletop because I have a prediction.

500
00:32:47,770 --> 00:32:52,870
Rym: MAGFest and PAX Unplugged were fully realized this time around.

501
00:32:53,191 --> 00:32:58,050
Rym: And PAX Unplugged was like way overcrowded as people return from the dark times.

502
00:32:58,770 --> 00:33:02,009
Rym: I think this PAX East is going to be the most crowded PAX East we've seen.

503
00:33:02,850 --> 00:33:08,410
Scott: So, I agree that PAX East attendee-wise is going to be off the hook, right?

504
00:33:08,993 --> 00:33:10,809
Scott: There's going to be so many attendees there.

505
00:33:11,291 --> 00:33:17,030
Scott: However, I believe that the industry, they'll be there for sure.

506
00:33:17,492 --> 00:33:18,830
Rym: I agree with what you're saying.

507
00:33:19,570 --> 00:33:28,050
Scott: The industry will not be back to its peak level that it was at in terms of convention presence, and I don't know if it ever will be.

508
00:33:28,431 --> 00:33:31,680
Scott: E3 died, and I don't think they're looking to replace E3.

509
00:33:31,780 --> 00:33:34,227
Scott: I think they're just saying, "We don't need something like

510
00:33:34,267 --> 00:33:34,428
Scott: that.".

511
00:33:36,752 --> 00:33:41,090
Scott: Apple has been very happy to not have people come to their auditorium and just do videos.

512
00:33:41,570 --> 00:33:43,710
Scott: Nintendo has been very happy to do Nintendo Direct.

513
00:33:44,751 --> 00:33:45,393
Scott: And you know what?

514
00:33:45,474 --> 00:33:47,521
Rym: I think this is good for video games.

515
00:33:48,344 --> 00:33:49,629
Rym: We've had waves of...

516
00:33:49,971 --> 00:33:52,870
Scott: And every industry that has in-person trade shows.

517
00:33:53,910 --> 00:34:00,464
Rym: I think it'll be good because over time, what we could see is the conventions start to return to the roots of.

518
00:34:00,805 --> 00:34:03,350
Rym: players are there to interact primarily with each other.

519
00:34:03,730 --> 00:34:12,909
Rym: Maybe to interact with some indie devs, but they're not there to see the spectacle of the six-figure Nintendo booth that is basically just a tiny attraction/ad.

520
00:34:15,210 --> 00:34:28,290
Scott: I think that industry in general, if you look at other shows that have survived, like CES or various photo shows, it's like they really are what E3 was originally supposed to be, which is, yeah.

521
00:34:28,409 --> 00:34:37,690
Scott: The only people that come here are people in the media, reviewers, press, other industry people, and it's totally insider event.

522
00:34:37,770 --> 00:34:43,330
Scott: And even though some nerds might really want to go to look at all the new whatever, they don't, right?

523
00:34:43,552 --> 00:34:45,250
Scott: Because that's just not how it works.

524
00:34:45,431 --> 00:34:49,550
Rym: It's funny, there was just an article on Kotaku that I didn't read yet, but it's in an open tab.

525
00:34:49,750 --> 00:35:02,270
Rym: And it's a whole bunch of documentary photos and descriptions of the absolutely unhinged early 2000s era video game convention and video game event marketing.

526
00:35:03,831 --> 00:35:09,830
Rym: Which was an order of magnitude more insane than what we saw at PAX's up until the dark times.

527
00:35:12,912 --> 00:35:21,150
Rym: So yeah, otherwise, beyond PAX East, I think we got nothing big until PAX West, and then PAX Unplugged again, and then MagFest again.

528
00:35:22,451 --> 00:35:25,650
Scott: Maybe we'll find another thing to do somewhere along the way.

529
00:35:28,152 --> 00:35:32,164
Scott: In a minor news, I've ordered the Geeknight sticker.

530
00:35:32,184 --> 00:35:34,110
Scott: The stickers, they're coming.

531
00:35:35,311 --> 00:35:40,062
Scott: Apparently they had a delay because they were moving their printers, and I'm like, "I really don't care.

532
00:35:40,663 --> 00:35:42,808
Scott: I just want to make sure I didn't get forgotten because I paid

533
00:35:42,849 --> 00:35:43,049
Scott: money.".

534
00:35:44,075 --> 00:35:46,050
Scott: I'm not in a hurry, it's not for business or anything.

535
00:35:46,810 --> 00:35:54,150
Scott: And so those stickers are currently being QA inspected by whoever, and they'll come to my house.

536
00:35:54,850 --> 00:36:01,469
Scott: And so I guess if you want a Geeknight sticker, I guess I'll post a photo of it at some point so you can see if you want it.

537
00:36:02,272 --> 00:36:07,390
Scott: And then you can, you know, come to our communities and ask for one, I guess.

538
00:36:07,790 --> 00:36:10,526
Rym: Maybe first, find us at PAX East and...

539
00:36:11,371 --> 00:36:13,263
Scott: Yeah, there was 100% chance.

540
00:36:13,303 --> 00:36:14,390
Scott: I'm going to have them at PAX East.

541
00:36:14,810 --> 00:36:16,470
Rym: If you find us at PAX East and you have a sticker...

542
00:36:16,610 --> 00:36:23,750
Scott: But remember, one of the rules of PAX East, there's literally only less than 10 rules, and one of them is don't put stickers on shit that isn't yours.

543
00:36:24,390 --> 00:36:30,570
Rym: If I see a fucking Geeknight sticker stuck somewhere it shouldn't be at PAX, I'm going to try to track you down and get you in trouble.

544
00:36:31,395 --> 00:36:31,849
Scott: That's right.

545
00:36:33,190 --> 00:36:39,729
Rym: Like, I don't think we'll find that many of you, so if we give away five stickers and I see a sticker stuck somewhere, it's one of you five, and we're coming.

546
00:36:41,296 --> 00:36:41,547
Rym: That's right.

547
00:36:43,052 --> 00:36:45,590
Scott: As long as you can stick it outside of PAX, though.

548
00:36:46,010 --> 00:36:51,250
Rym: As long as PAX does not get in trouble, we do not get in trouble, I could not care less what you do with our stickers.

549
00:36:51,390 --> 00:36:53,585
Scott: Right, if you stick that sticker out on the streets of Boston, sure.

550
00:36:54,430 --> 00:36:54,770
Scott: Godspeed.

551
00:36:56,856 --> 00:36:58,430
Scott: On your own ass, that's yours.

552
00:36:58,450 --> 00:36:59,768
Scott: Just don't mess up PAX.

553
00:37:00,730 --> 00:37:04,250
Scott: Right, on the convention center, on someone's booth, no, not acceptable.

554
00:37:05,770 --> 00:37:13,130
Rym: Alright, so our panel, the one we're going to do at PAX East, is broadly around the idea that all games are the same game.

555
00:37:13,250 --> 00:37:27,010
Rym: Scott had this idea, we kind of riffed on it a little bit, and there's a lot we could cover, but one of the things we were kind of discussing is the idea that you can take any game and start to distill it into combinations of other games.

556
00:37:28,434 --> 00:37:33,250
Scott: I love a very reductionist approach to everything.

557
00:37:33,670 --> 00:37:43,610
Rym: Yeah, we spent 20 minutes being as reductionist as possible on what is a game mechanic in our last panel, so of course, here, what we're going to get down to is physics a game mechanic.

558
00:37:44,430 --> 00:37:48,961
Scott: Right, so obviously not every game is the same game, right?

559
00:37:49,001 --> 00:37:52,510
Scott: In fact, how many times do we say the exact opposite?

560
00:37:52,590 --> 00:37:57,970
Scott: We're like, oh, you changed one rule in baseball, it's a different game now, it's not the same game anymore, right?

561
00:37:58,090 --> 00:38:00,317
Rym: I stand by both of those ideas, too.

562
00:38:00,960 --> 00:38:04,090
Rym: Cognitive dissonance, I don't care, I'm a human, I can do whatever I want in my brain.

563
00:38:04,290 --> 00:38:09,438
Scott: But, you know, you can take any game, though, and break it into its component games, right?

564
00:38:09,478 --> 00:38:17,350
Scott: In some cases, it's really obvious, like Mario Party, it's like, okay, there's the board part, but then there's these mini games that are obviously separate games.

565
00:38:17,490 --> 00:38:25,642
Scott: But if you break apart baseball, it's like, okay, well, there's the game of, can you throw the ball so that the other guy can't hit it?

566
00:38:26,123 --> 00:38:30,890
Scott: Can you hit the ball that is going so fast that someone's trying to not let you hit it?

567
00:38:31,150 --> 00:38:33,047
Rym: There's the base running game, there's the outfield game.

568
00:38:33,490 --> 00:38:35,833
Scott: It's like, can you run fast?

569
00:38:35,934 --> 00:38:44,886
Scott: It's like, okay, so it's this hitting game, throwing game, plus running game, plus somewhat strategy game, right?

570
00:38:44,926 --> 00:38:47,330
Scott: Where do you stand and stuff like that, where do you throw to?

571
00:38:49,091 --> 00:38:54,559
Scott: It's like, there's all these, every different part of it is sort of its own mini game.

572
00:38:54,599 --> 00:39:00,990
Scott: You could play just baseball without any base running, and it would still be, that's a game you could have, right?

573
00:39:01,593 --> 00:39:04,970
Scott: You could play just base running with no balls and no bats.

574
00:39:05,450 --> 00:39:10,240
Rym: Because sometimes there was only like two or three of us, so we couldn't play like baseball, baseball.

575
00:39:10,561 --> 00:39:13,066
Rym: So we'd play a mini version where there wasn't really base running.

576
00:39:13,107 --> 00:39:14,670
Rym: It was just pitching, throwing, and catching.

577
00:39:15,371 --> 00:39:18,135
Scott: Yep, it's like you can play those, right?

578
00:39:18,195 --> 00:39:28,490
Scott: So if you look at most video games especially, and board games that us nerds are playing, it's like they break apart pretty clearly into separate games.

579
00:39:28,650 --> 00:39:33,798
Scott: It's like, ah yes, I can see here that, you know, Mario, right?

580
00:39:34,219 --> 00:39:40,850
Scott: It's this sort of platformy thing, but there's also sort of a can you beat the monsters kind of thing going on, right?

581
00:39:40,850 --> 00:39:43,754
Scott: And sometimes you get a puzzle level going on, right?

582
00:39:44,094 --> 00:39:45,476
Scott: It's like, can you solve this?

583
00:39:45,496 --> 00:39:49,361
Scott: You know, can you find the secret in this level?

584
00:39:49,682 --> 00:39:51,625
Scott: Sometimes there's a time challenge.

585
00:39:52,065 --> 00:39:55,630
Scott: Sometimes, you know, it's all kinds of different games going on.

586
00:39:55,710 --> 00:39:58,475
Scott: Each level could be considered an entirely separate game.

587
00:40:00,037 --> 00:40:07,790
Scott: But when you get right down to it, if you want to do ultra ultra reductionism, there are actually only a handful of games.

588
00:40:09,074 --> 00:40:13,270
Scott: I haven't listed all of them because I don't feel like I've figured out all of them.

589
00:40:13,450 --> 00:40:17,529
Rym: But I feel like your list already covers like every game I can think of.

590
00:40:18,450 --> 00:40:18,830
Scott: Yeah, right?

591
00:40:18,891 --> 00:40:21,514
Scott: It's like there's random game, right?

592
00:40:21,534 --> 00:40:27,481
Scott: So, you know, the question here is, is War, coin toss, and candy land?

593
00:40:27,721 --> 00:40:29,183
Scott: Those are clearly different games.

594
00:40:29,263 --> 00:40:30,124
Scott: They have different rules.

595
00:40:30,745 --> 00:40:35,070
Scott: But all of them boil down to, we randomly select a winner.

596
00:40:35,593 --> 00:40:35,774
Rym: Yep.

597
00:40:36,298 --> 00:40:37,043
Rym: There's nothing else.

598
00:40:38,990 --> 00:40:43,568
Scott: If you're going to play randomly, if you're going to play Candy Land, why wouldn't you just play coin toss instead?

599
00:40:43,648 --> 00:40:44,170
Scott: It's faster.

600
00:40:44,770 --> 00:40:46,995
Rym: Now, there is actually a good answer to that.

601
00:40:47,135 --> 00:40:47,516
Rym: why.

602
00:40:47,917 --> 00:40:54,430
Rym: And that might be something you bring up in the panel because the answer to the why is, Candy Land teaches the concept of move your mice.

603
00:40:54,851 --> 00:40:56,815
Rym: It teaches you concepts of colors and numbers.

604
00:40:57,196 --> 00:40:58,940
Rym: It teaches you concepts of winning and losing.

605
00:40:59,300 --> 00:41:03,690
Rym: And then for the smart kid, it teaches you that this game is bullshit and there's nothing I can do.

606
00:41:04,571 --> 00:41:08,376
Scott: But mechanically, mathematically, Candy Land is no different.

607
00:41:08,396 --> 00:41:17,950
Scott: Playing Candy Land by the rules in terms of the winning and the losing part, the ortho game part, is the same as coin toss, game wise.

608
00:41:18,830 --> 00:41:22,476
Scott: And so the second game is, you know, rock, paper, scissors, right?

609
00:41:23,197 --> 00:41:26,842
Scott: Is rock, paper, scissors any different from poison in the cup?

610
00:41:27,864 --> 00:41:32,070
Scott: Or is it any different from, you know, three card Monty without cheating?

611
00:41:32,311 --> 00:41:41,550
Rym: I feel like because we've often in older panels, we would talk about there's only three kinds of games and we called them random, arbitrary or predetermined.

612
00:41:42,531 --> 00:41:42,731
Scott: Yeah.

613
00:41:44,416 --> 00:41:46,381
Scott: So it's like what's, you know, it's like sort of like.

614
00:41:46,401 --> 00:41:49,730
Scott: there's a whole bunch of games, you know, that are boiled down to rock, paper, scissors.

615
00:41:49,990 --> 00:41:51,092
Scott: Yeah.

616
00:41:51,172 --> 00:41:59,226
Scott: Or there are games that even if they don't boil down to rock, paper, scissors are, you know, a large components of them are rock, paper, scissors, fighting games, right?

617
00:41:59,266 --> 00:42:01,770
Scott: When you choose characters, that's a rock, paper, scissors part.

618
00:42:02,231 --> 00:42:09,630
Scott: And then in the fighting game itself, it's basically a series of rock, paper, scissors battles right over over an amount of time.

619
00:42:09,730 --> 00:42:12,097
Rym: Or it's a series of rock, paper, scissors battles.

620
00:42:12,137 --> 00:42:16,490
Rym: But there's also an interleaved series of dexterity challenges.

621
00:42:16,771 --> 00:42:17,113
Rym: Yes.

622
00:42:17,374 --> 00:42:17,595
Scott: Right.

623
00:42:17,615 --> 00:42:20,349
Scott: But that's those are the components that make up a fighting game.

624
00:42:20,770 --> 00:42:21,332
Rym: Exactly.

625
00:42:21,412 --> 00:42:24,361
Scott: And this random game, rock, paper, scissors game, right?

626
00:42:24,843 --> 00:42:25,746
Scott: Vote who wins game.

627
00:42:25,786 --> 00:42:27,070
Scott: We've talked about that a whole bunch.

628
00:42:27,410 --> 00:42:27,572
Rym: Yeah.

629
00:42:27,592 --> 00:42:28,929
Rym: The Richard Garfield political game.

630
00:42:29,910 --> 00:42:29,990
Scott: Yeah.

631
00:42:30,011 --> 00:42:30,794
Scott: Vote who wins game.

632
00:42:31,737 --> 00:42:34,930
Scott: Knowledge game, which is just do you know something?

633
00:42:35,510 --> 00:42:45,890
Scott: And do you know something could be, you know, literally a trivia game, but it could also be something like, you know, Pokemon.

634
00:42:46,150 --> 00:42:48,773
Scott: Clearly, Pokemon isn't 100 percent a knowledge game.

635
00:42:49,174 --> 00:43:01,890
Scott: But if you know more about Pokemon and like what moves the Pokemon could possibly have and what's strong against what and what Pokemon exist and and all these sorts of things, you have a strong advantage in Pokemon.

636
00:43:02,451 --> 00:43:09,370
Rym: So Pokemon, a large component of it is a trivia game because if someone does not have that knowledge, Sudowoodo is going to fucking trick you.

637
00:43:10,150 --> 00:43:10,471
Scott: That's right.

638
00:43:10,531 --> 00:43:16,630
Scott: It's like, you know, and if you play Magic the Gathering, it's just like knowing all the cards that your opponent could play.

639
00:43:16,931 --> 00:43:19,320
Scott: You don't know what's in their deck, but you could have knowledge.

640
00:43:19,541 --> 00:43:21,910
Scott: And if you have that knowledge, that would give you a strong advantage.

641
00:43:22,511 --> 00:43:24,077
Scott: It's largely a knowledge game.

642
00:43:24,257 --> 00:43:24,418
Scott: Right.

643
00:43:24,438 --> 00:43:27,570
Scott: Do you know the rules and do you know all the things that could happen?

644
00:43:28,533 --> 00:43:34,130
Rym: Now, knowledge might be a test of free time, preparation and or wealth.

645
00:43:34,971 --> 00:43:35,473
Scott: It could be.

646
00:43:35,693 --> 00:43:35,934
Scott: Right.

647
00:43:36,796 --> 00:43:37,378
Scott: Indirectly.

648
00:43:37,619 --> 00:43:37,799
Scott: Right.

649
00:43:37,819 --> 00:43:41,449
Scott: But there are some games that could literally be a test of wealth.

650
00:43:41,549 --> 00:43:41,730
Scott: Right.

651
00:43:42,771 --> 00:43:50,810
Scott: If you look at horse racing from the perspective of like the owner, that could be like richest guy gets the best horses.

652
00:43:51,230 --> 00:43:56,450
Rym: Or casino, having more money gives you better odds of coming out with more money than you came in with.

653
00:43:56,571 --> 00:43:59,389
Scott: Having more money means you can own a casino instead of playing one.

654
00:44:02,672 --> 00:44:07,044
Scott: You know, but there are some games that are literally whoever has the most resources wins.

655
00:44:07,124 --> 00:44:07,305
Scott: Right.

656
00:44:07,345 --> 00:44:09,210
Scott: Because, you know, I have better equipment.

657
00:44:09,510 --> 00:44:10,893
Scott: I have better training.

658
00:44:11,214 --> 00:44:15,244
Scott: You know, for race car racing, I just bought a better car than you do.

659
00:44:15,264 --> 00:44:17,690
Scott: I raced my Ferrari against your Toyota.

660
00:44:17,810 --> 00:44:18,111
Scott: I win.

661
00:44:18,131 --> 00:44:22,381
Scott: You know, there are games like that.

662
00:44:22,882 --> 00:44:25,769
Scott: There is the and I guess there's just sport.

663
00:44:26,170 --> 00:44:26,350
Scott: Right.

664
00:44:26,450 --> 00:44:31,601
Scott: Which is, you know, there's obviously a large variety within sport, but it's they're all.

665
00:44:31,882 --> 00:44:35,470
Scott: if you are reductionist, simply testing the human body.

666
00:44:35,711 --> 00:44:44,650
Scott: Is your human body better at a particular measurement than someone else's human body in this very particular way?

667
00:44:44,970 --> 00:44:48,205
Scott: We're going to, you know, arm wrestle.

668
00:44:48,225 --> 00:44:49,390
Scott: Who's got the best forearm?

669
00:44:49,730 --> 00:44:54,990
Rym: One of our old panels where we talked about fast and frugal heuristics and how to take your turn and also how to win.

670
00:44:55,551 --> 00:45:00,450
Rym: Then some of those tests are also testing brain, but they're not testing knowledge.

671
00:45:00,650 --> 00:45:05,782
Rym: They're testing a similar concept of either reflexes or hockey.

672
00:45:05,822 --> 00:45:09,330
Rym: sense is a term that comes up a lot lately in the commentary on hockey games.

673
00:45:10,511 --> 00:45:21,690
Rym: Like we're testing that there's all these other like sub tests that eventually all boil down to which biological mechanical system of the human body are we testing?

674
00:45:22,491 --> 00:45:29,510
Scott: We have created an arbitrary measuring stick and we will line up your human body against that measuring stick and take a reading.

675
00:45:29,932 --> 00:45:33,090
Scott: And then we will take someone else's reading and see who's is higher.

676
00:45:33,410 --> 00:45:36,701
Rym: Some of those things a human could take actions to get better at.

677
00:45:37,063 --> 00:45:39,130
Rym: And some of those things, there's nothing you can do.

678
00:45:39,792 --> 00:45:42,942
Scott: And some of those things vary by day and by the minute.

679
00:45:43,122 --> 00:45:45,530
Scott: We can take a set of measurements over time.

680
00:45:46,111 --> 00:45:50,627
Scott: Over the course of the next nine innings of this baseball game or the 60 minutes of this hockey game.

681
00:45:51,550 --> 00:45:52,889
Scott: We're going to take readings constantly.

682
00:45:53,490 --> 00:45:58,430
Rym: A pitcher's first pitch is definitely not going to be the same as their 15th pitch if they even get to that many pitches.

683
00:45:59,512 --> 00:46:01,129
Scott: They get to 15 pitches, no problem.

684
00:46:02,731 --> 00:46:08,410
Scott: And then the, you know, the, the, the, I guess the biggest kind of game is just strategy game.

685
00:46:08,791 --> 00:46:09,012
Scott: Right.

686
00:46:09,072 --> 00:46:14,850
Scott: Which is, you know, usually the perfect information kinds, you know, your chess is your checkers, your right go.

687
00:46:15,511 --> 00:46:22,910
Scott: And it's like, sure, some of them have been solved like checkers, some of them not like chess, and some of them really not like go.

688
00:46:23,692 --> 00:46:25,138
Scott: So they're different in that sense.

689
00:46:25,279 --> 00:46:27,730
Scott: But in another sense, they're not different at all.

690
00:46:28,011 --> 00:46:30,387
Scott: They're, they're just the same game in different shapes.

691
00:46:31,671 --> 00:46:40,850
Scott: It's can you recognize the pattern right on this, this tree of possibilities and you can influence the path.

692
00:46:41,151 --> 00:46:44,707
Scott: And can you influence the path to a winning path or not?

693
00:46:45,930 --> 00:46:56,850
Rym: And it's interesting because many games that humans historically have seen across many cultures as long standing or important or culturally significant games.

694
00:46:57,211 --> 00:47:05,310
Rym: Tend to either be completely random or tend to effectively be pattern recognition games of varying complexity like chess checkers and go.

695
00:47:06,410 --> 00:47:16,269
Rym: But humans are both extremely specifically good at pattern recognition and extremely specifically bad at pattern recognition and that we do it too much.

696
00:47:18,550 --> 00:47:20,616
Rym: And AIs are starting to do the same thing.

697
00:47:20,656 --> 00:47:25,650
Rym: Like an AI hallucinating is not that different from a human seeing a face in a piece of toast.

698
00:47:27,951 --> 00:47:29,375
Rym: So there's so much to cover here.

699
00:47:29,395 --> 00:47:35,130
Rym: And another concept we were thinking about is the idea of, you know, in school, many of you, I assume, went to school.

700
00:47:35,631 --> 00:47:39,050
Rym: If you're listening to peak nights, some sort of education, whatever form it took.

701
00:47:39,330 --> 00:47:47,590
Rym: And you had to factorize an equation, meaning you take a complex equation and you start breaking it up into simpler components.

702
00:47:48,392 --> 00:47:48,653
Scott: Yeah.

703
00:47:48,714 --> 00:47:51,509
Scott: So we we hated doing that.

704
00:47:51,790 --> 00:47:56,790
Scott: And so we we wrote a program in the basic language of the TI 86 calculator.

705
00:47:56,990 --> 00:48:00,338
Rym: I think we both did the same thing despite not knowing each other yet.

706
00:48:00,358 --> 00:48:05,210
Rym: And I feel like a high percentage of all the nerds with the other amongst the nerdy kids.

707
00:48:05,551 --> 00:48:11,490
Scott: And then the teachers wanted to see that our calculator was erased before the test because we're allowed to use the calculator.

708
00:48:11,630 --> 00:48:17,450
Rym: So we shared around the app that looks at an app that just printed the erase on the screen.

709
00:48:18,610 --> 00:48:20,861
Scott: And it looked like it was erased, but it wasn't.

710
00:48:21,022 --> 00:48:22,510
Scott: And then I used the app on the test.

711
00:48:24,230 --> 00:48:36,750
Rym: But factorization, I want to say for complex equations, edges into like hard mathematical problem, because sometimes it can only be done by trial and error, depending on the complexity.

712
00:48:37,770 --> 00:48:53,950
Rym: But the fundamental idea that we want to try to apply to games and maybe we'll do this to a couple of games as an example in the panel is just start arbitrarily breaking a game down and follow some of the threads to break all the way down to a handful of very simple mechanics.

713
00:48:54,831 --> 00:49:15,150
Rym: So an example would be Cobalt Core, a game we reviewed recently, which we could easily say, if we simplify a little bit, is fundamentally a combination of FTL the game, Faster Than Light, crossed with Slay the Spire the game, and then plus a bunch of other small games that we're not going to worry about in our factorization.

714
00:49:15,230 --> 00:49:16,532
Rym: But those are like the two main games.

715
00:49:17,113 --> 00:49:28,550
Rym: But Slay the Spire can be broken down to effectively a deck builder crossed with a single player puzzle game, crossed with a roguelike, like a dungeon cross.

716
00:49:28,690 --> 00:49:34,003
Scott: And it's also a knowledge game because, you know, you have to know all the possible enemies.

717
00:49:34,043 --> 00:49:36,870
Scott: There's only a certain number of them and you need to know what cards are possible.

718
00:49:37,511 --> 00:49:46,303
Rym: We could take Slay the Spire itself and break it down to Dominion, which was arguably the first deck builder.

719
00:49:46,323 --> 00:49:51,770
Rym: I cannot think of a game that was even close to what we think of as a modern deck builder before Dominion.

720
00:49:51,770 --> 00:49:52,330
Scott: First one that mattered.

721
00:49:53,052 --> 00:49:54,885
Rym: Also still the best one, I think.

722
00:49:57,631 --> 00:50:01,077
Scott: It's too hard to figure out these days who is really the first.

723
00:50:01,558 --> 00:50:07,990
Scott: I do want to give credit to someone if they were really the first and they're not getting credit because someone stole their thing from them.

724
00:50:08,590 --> 00:50:12,428
Scott: But on the other hand, there's also recognize the first one that mattered too.

725
00:50:13,250 --> 00:50:15,010
Rym: Dominion was definitely the first one that mattered.

726
00:50:15,410 --> 00:50:19,440
Scott: It's like, yeah, Thomas Edison fucking sucks.

727
00:50:19,480 --> 00:50:20,944
Scott: Did he really invent the light bulb?

728
00:50:21,285 --> 00:50:21,686
Scott: Whatever.

729
00:50:21,866 --> 00:50:23,390
Scott: But it was the one that mattered.

730
00:50:23,510 --> 00:50:34,150
Rym: So even if Boss was technically the first worker placement game we know of, Kalis is the first one that mattered because Kalis is when the whole board gaming world started talking about worker placement as a concept.

731
00:50:35,870 --> 00:50:37,092
Rym: But we could then.

732
00:50:37,292 --> 00:50:51,230
Rym: so you can break complex games down into a collection of simpler games, but then you can break each one of those games down into a collection of mechanics and they can break those mechanics down to Scott's very short list.

733
00:50:53,211 --> 00:51:04,850
Rym: And I have been idly starting to factorize Cobalt Core and you can go pretty deep and do a very long exercise if you do this.

734
00:51:06,571 --> 00:51:13,010
Scott: It's like any game you think of is like, you know, and sometimes games really are like literally the same.

735
00:51:13,210 --> 00:51:19,042
Scott: It's like you look at something like, you know, Civ 5 and Civ 6 and it's like, sure, it's.

736
00:51:19,363 --> 00:51:23,030
Scott: it's like there are differences between them, right?

737
00:51:23,050 --> 00:51:24,073
Scott: They're not the same game.

738
00:51:24,254 --> 00:51:24,895
Scott: You don't right.

739
00:51:24,915 --> 00:51:25,256
Scott: They're not.

740
00:51:25,718 --> 00:51:27,824
Scott: it's not like, oh, yes, I would always play one.

741
00:51:27,864 --> 00:51:29,850
Scott: The other one, there's no reason for it to exist anymore.

742
00:51:30,051 --> 00:51:31,535
Scott: The rules are significantly different.

743
00:51:31,555 --> 00:51:32,718
Scott: They are very different games.

744
00:51:33,180 --> 00:51:36,810
Scott: But if you factorize them, it's like the same ingredients.

745
00:51:36,930 --> 00:51:37,151
Scott: Yep.

746
00:51:37,411 --> 00:51:37,592
Scott: Right.

747
00:51:37,872 --> 00:51:38,153
Scott: It's like.

748
00:51:38,394 --> 00:51:45,670
Scott: it's like you could take the same ingredients and make two recipes, but in different proportions of the ingredients and different preparations of them.

749
00:51:46,251 --> 00:51:47,414
Scott: But the ingredients are the same.

750
00:51:48,316 --> 00:51:53,710
Scott: And so basically what it's like what we're saying in the panel is that it's not that every game is the same.

751
00:51:53,790 --> 00:51:54,832
Scott: It's the opposite of that.

752
00:51:54,952 --> 00:52:05,010
Scott: It's that there is not that many ingredients in making games and all games are basically made from the same very short list of ingredients in different combinations and amounts and flavors.

753
00:52:05,110 --> 00:52:05,230
Rym: Yep.

754
00:52:05,691 --> 00:52:29,970
Rym: And I think it also means I have found trying to factorize Cobalt Core, there is a lot of value in thinking about games this way, even though I've definitely encountered a large number of game designers and developers over the years who get really mad when you describe their game as other game crossed with other other game, like they get really mad about that.

755
00:52:30,690 --> 00:52:38,230
Scott: But I think, you know, if you're if you're trying to sell a game, you know, if you're making a work of art, right, you don't want to, you know, even outside of games.

756
00:52:38,511 --> 00:52:40,096
Scott: But, you know, you don't want to be.

757
00:52:40,116 --> 00:52:44,270
Scott: if you're making music, you don't want someone to say, oh, you're just Nirvana plus a little bit of that.

758
00:52:44,710 --> 00:52:44,871
Scott: But at

759
00:52:44,891 --> 00:53:03,290
Rym: the same time, people who study games and also people like us, if someone describes a game as these three games crossed together, that is usually way more useful, interesting and valuable to me than them trying to describe the game as its own like equation or its own like acting like it's a prime number.

760
00:53:03,911 --> 00:53:04,091
Scott: Right.

761
00:53:04,131 --> 00:53:11,064
Scott: But people don't want to be seen as, you know, unoriginal, uncreative, just copying or they also don't want.

762
00:53:11,425 --> 00:53:14,390
Scott: And also the other thing people don't want is from a marketing perspective.

763
00:53:14,771 --> 00:53:14,951
Scott: Right.

764
00:53:15,011 --> 00:53:24,270
Scott: It's like if you say that my music is like, you know, Nirvana plus Queen, then people who don't like Nirvana or don't like Queen are going to not even look at my game.

765
00:53:24,410 --> 00:53:33,790
Scott: Even though if you tell me your game and people who do like Nirvana and or Queen might come in with twisted expectations now and also bounce off.

766
00:53:33,910 --> 00:53:34,191
Scott: True.

767
00:53:34,431 --> 00:53:44,290
Rym: But simultaneously, if you tell me this game is World of Warcraft crossed with anything, as long as that statement is accurate, now I don't have to bounce off.

768
00:53:44,430 --> 00:53:45,339
Rym: I know to avoid it.

769
00:53:46,681 --> 00:53:47,021
Scott: Right.

770
00:53:47,382 --> 00:53:51,748
Scott: It's it's good information if it's accurate for, you know, enjoyers of artwork.

771
00:53:51,808 --> 00:53:59,700
Scott: But I'm saying creators of artwork from their perspective have very good reason to not like that kind of conversation out in the world.

772
00:54:00,561 --> 00:54:00,781
Rym: Yep.

773
00:54:01,162 --> 00:54:10,580
Rym: But Tycho and Penny Arcade recently wrote something, not going to pull the whole thing up, but he was talking about PAL World and the somewhat...

774
00:54:10,740 --> 00:54:14,080
Scott: PAL World, which is literally just the salad of every game ever in one space.

775
00:54:14,100 --> 00:54:20,540
Rym: But he was responding to the kind of extreme vitriol that's been growing from a subset of gamers about that game.

776
00:54:22,261 --> 00:54:43,340
Rym: And to summarize what he said, that sort of like remix mashing together of existing popular things into something else is a very important function of gaming in that it is synthesis like that that leads to patterns and leads to memory and like the long term development of games.

777
00:54:44,701 --> 00:54:53,680
Scott: Well, the point we're making with the panel is that whether you are trying to or not, games are always synthesis because there aren't that many ingredients.

778
00:54:53,920 --> 00:54:59,240
Scott: It's not, you know, like I use the food analogy, but with food, there's so many ingredients.

779
00:54:59,360 --> 00:55:06,740
Rym: Of course, there's so many ingredients that there's food, there's so many ingredients, but there's a. there is a limited number of atoms.

780
00:55:08,760 --> 00:55:10,520
Rym: There's a limited number of types of atoms.

781
00:55:10,560 --> 00:55:10,881
Rym: There aren't.

782
00:55:10,921 --> 00:55:17,360
Scott: But the point is that with food, with atoms, there are hundreds of kinds of atoms, tens that are edible.

783
00:55:17,780 --> 00:55:22,560
Rym: But those atoms break down because of the molecules and molecules change the flavor.

784
00:55:22,660 --> 00:55:26,731
Scott: But the point is that with games, there aren't really.

785
00:55:26,792 --> 00:55:29,820
Scott: it's like the periodic table of games is like one row.

786
00:55:30,069 --> 00:55:30,257
Rym: Yep.

787
00:55:30,802 --> 00:55:31,367
Scott: And that's it.

788
00:55:31,589 --> 00:55:32,900
Scott: It's not like a whole table.

789
00:55:33,001 --> 00:55:33,485
Scott: It's a row.

790
00:55:34,111 --> 00:55:34,918
Scott: It's a little baggy.

791
00:55:37,043 --> 00:55:39,600
Scott: There's not too many choices when it comes to game.

792
00:55:40,141 --> 00:55:49,820
Rym: Though, to borrow some of your language, the same set of mechanics mixed together in different proportions and with different bonds and interactions between each other.

793
00:55:49,840 --> 00:55:50,820
Scott: They get a different flavor.

794
00:55:51,000 --> 00:55:52,200
Rym: They have a very different flavor.

795
00:55:52,420 --> 00:55:53,220
Rym: It means that's right.

796
00:55:53,340 --> 00:55:57,340
Rym: This is yet another panel where I get to use my favorite word brain feel.

797
00:56:00,341 --> 00:56:05,480
Scott: It's like, yeah, you've only got like if you only had seven atoms to work with, how many molecules could you make?

798
00:56:05,900 --> 00:56:06,479
Scott: Still infinity.

799
00:56:07,900 --> 00:56:08,381
Scott: Because you can.

800
00:56:08,421 --> 00:56:17,260
Scott: still it's like, yeah, six oxygens, seven oxygens, eight oxygens, nine oxygens in a row to benzene rings, three benzene ring.

801
00:56:17,440 --> 00:56:19,500
Rym: It's like sort of because then you know instability.

802
00:56:19,622 --> 00:56:21,100
Rym: But games had a similar instability.

803
00:56:21,401 --> 00:56:23,680
Rym: Too many rules together turned into advanced squad leader.

804
00:56:24,222 --> 00:56:28,460
Scott: And even with just one kind of, you know, Adam, it's like, OK, I just got water to work with.

805
00:56:28,803 --> 00:56:29,307
Scott: I got ice.

806
00:56:29,327 --> 00:56:30,740
Scott: I got water.

807
00:56:31,225 --> 00:56:32,200
Scott: I got water vapor.

808
00:56:32,763 --> 00:56:35,120
Scott: I got steam that's, you know, blasting.

809
00:56:35,440 --> 00:56:36,800
Scott: I got, you know, plasma.

810
00:56:36,942 --> 00:56:38,159
Scott: Theoretically, I don't fucking know.

811
00:56:38,360 --> 00:56:43,580
Scott: You got at the same time with ice crystals and then the sort of fuzzy ice crystals.

812
00:56:44,220 --> 00:56:45,180
Rym: Bog is standard.

813
00:56:45,360 --> 00:56:54,860
Rym: Trick taking with the slightest of variations has led to hundreds and hundreds and hundreds of very unique games that are all fundamentally the same fucking game.

814
00:56:54,900 --> 00:56:56,980
Rym: And yet they play so radically differently.

815
00:56:57,840 --> 00:56:57,965
Scott: Mm hmm.

816
00:56:58,623 --> 00:56:59,919
Scott: I feel like it's going to be a good panel of them.

817
00:57:00,220 --> 00:57:02,020
Scott: Some of them fun and most of them not.

818
00:57:02,040 --> 00:57:02,148
Scott: Yep.

819
00:57:05,046 --> 00:57:05,939
Rym: Looking at you, Cribbage.

820
00:57:12,010 --> 00:57:14,310
Rym: This has been GeekNights with Rym and Scott.

821
00:57:14,470 --> 00:57:16,970
Rym: Special thanks to DJ Pretzel for the opening theme.

822
00:57:17,170 --> 00:57:23,170
Scott: Be sure to visit our website at FrontRowCoup.com for show notes, discussion, links and more.

823
00:57:23,670 --> 00:57:26,310
Rym: Remember, GeekNights is not one, but four different shows.

824
00:57:26,670 --> 00:57:28,189
Rym: Mondays are science and technology.

825
00:57:28,370 --> 00:57:30,050
Rym: Tuesdays, gaming and games.

826
00:57:30,250 --> 00:57:31,969
Rym: Wednesdays are animation and comics.

827
00:57:32,410 --> 00:57:34,770
Rym: Thursdays are arbitrary and indiscriminate.

828
00:57:34,830 --> 00:57:40,410
Rym: GeekNights is distributed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License.

829
00:57:40,930 --> 00:57:45,150
Scott: GeekNights is recorded live with no studio and no audience.

830
00:57:45,710 --> 00:57:49,130
Scott: But unlike those other late shows, it's actually recorded at night.

831
00:57:49,730 --> 00:58:04,929
Rym: And the Patreon patrons for this episode of GeekNights are I will not say at their explicit request.

832
00:58:05,630 --> 00:58:09,250
Rym: Tonight, back from skiing and got some hockey tomorrow.

833
00:58:11,273 --> 00:58:13,750
Rym: Really no conventions or anything big until PAX East.

834
00:58:14,371 --> 00:58:21,569
Rym: So I'm just going to leave you with... All Tom Hanks wanted was a quiet vacation at home.

835
00:58:21,770 --> 00:58:22,918
Rym: This is what I need, Carol.

836
00:58:23,180 --> 00:58:24,469
Rym: I need this.

837
00:58:26,272 --> 00:58:27,848
Rym: Welcome to Mayfield Place.

838
00:58:30,542 --> 00:58:32,230
Rym: A typical street in the 'burbs.

839
00:58:32,872 --> 00:58:33,508
Rym: Morning, Walter!

840
00:58:34,111 --> 00:58:35,508
Rym: Where nothing much ever happened.

841
00:58:35,910 --> 00:58:38,849
Scott: Walter's dog just took a dump on Rumsfeld's lawn again.

842
00:58:39,991 --> 00:58:41,669
Rym: Until the Clopex moved in.

843
00:58:41,891 --> 00:58:42,346
Rym: Clopex?

844
00:58:42,690 --> 00:58:43,124
Rym: Clopex.

845
00:58:43,491 --> 00:58:43,886
Rym: Clopex.

846
00:58:44,370 --> 00:58:46,489
Scott: No one goes in, no one comes out.

847
00:58:46,950 --> 00:58:47,769
Scott: Neighbors from hell.

848
00:58:49,293 --> 00:58:50,609
Rym: It was a nice place to live.

849
00:58:50,950 --> 00:58:54,890
Scott: He said he thinks the Clopex are evil incarnate.

850
00:58:55,812 --> 00:58:58,430
Scott: Well, you're much too smart to fall for that, aren't you, honey?

851
00:58:58,670 --> 00:58:59,577
Rym: But now... Carol!

852
00:59:02,254 --> 00:59:03,568
Rym: You wouldn't want to visit there.

853
00:59:04,635 --> 00:59:06,210
Rym: Ray, this is Walter.

854
00:59:06,791 --> 00:59:06,834
Rym: No!

855
00:59:10,623 --> 00:59:11,278
Rym: The 'burbs.

856
00:59:11,620 --> 00:59:15,320
Rym: I'm going over the fence, and I'm not coming back till I find a dead body.

857
00:59:17,948 --> 00:59:22,159
Rym: Ray, do you want them to take your family, kidnap them, tear their livers out, and make some kind of satanic pate?

858
00:59:24,845 --> 00:59:25,677
Rym: We found Walter!

859
00:59:26,083 --> 00:59:27,079
Rym: We got a real problem.

860
00:59:27,441 --> 00:59:28,778
Scott: I hit the gas line, I'd run!

861
00:59:31,171 --> 00:59:32,189
Scott: God, I love this street.

862
00:59:32,570 --> 00:59:33,288
Rym: Tom Hanks.

863
00:59:33,630 --> 00:59:34,370
Rym: I think we are overreacting.

864
00:59:35,330 --> 00:59:35,400
Scott: No.

865
00:59:35,990 --> 00:59:36,626
Scott: The 'burbs.

866
00:59:37,190 --> 00:59:38,730
Rym: It's one hell of a neighborhood.

867
00:59:39,350 --> 00:59:41,130
Rym: Hey, honey, I think we should move.

